#!/bin/sh
# Generated from package-actions/postinst.sh.in by
# scripts/patch-maintenance-launchd.py. Do not edit the installed copy.
#
# Why this is shell and not a compiled tool
# -----------------------------------------
# On roothide both the jbroot path redirection and the sandbox exemption come
# from basebin/bootstrap.dylib, injected through DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES. The
# reporting device ran a compiled maintainer script as euid 0 that could stat,
# read and parse files inside the jailbreak root but got EPERM on every write and
# on every child exec, and saw bare paths such as /bin/launchctl as ENOENT. That
# is a process-level restriction, not permissions and not a read-only mount.
#
# So the shell half owns everything that depends on being the redirected process:
# resolving the jailbreak root, running launchctl, and handing the root to a
# helper that is not redirected.
#
# Loading the job used to be the guard's job, and the reporting device showed why
# that cannot work. Every launchctl candidate the guard found came back from
# posix_spawn with EPERM (errno 1), including <jbroot>/usr/bin/launchctl, the real
# binary:
#
#   probed <jbroot>/bin/launchctl(spawn errno 1),
#          <jbroot>/usr/bin/launchctl(spawn errno 1);
#          18 other probed paths do not exist
#
# Two facts from that same dpkg run settle the cause. The guard saw every bare
# path as ENOENT while jbroot-absolute paths resolved, so it has no path
# redirection; and this shell exec'd both `jbroot` and the guard itself without
# trouble. The restriction is on the guard's process, not on the paths it tried,
# so no amount of probing inside the guard could have fixed it. The guard now
# reads and reports; the shell execs.
#
# What this script no longer does
# -------------------------------
# It used to rewrite the launchd plist, substituting a @JBROOT@ placeholder with
# the live jailbreak root, on the grounds that launchd is not redirected and so
# needs a real absolute path. That was wrong on roothide, and it was the cause of
# the doubled path the reporting device showed:
#
#   program = <jbroot>/<jbroot>/usr/libexec/nrmanager-maintenance
#
# roothide's launchctl is itself redirected and patches the plist on load. Its
# _patch_plist walks every path-bearing key and rewrites each value that starts
# with '/' as jbroot(value), guarding re-entry only with a __Patched flag it sets
# itself -- it never checks whether the value already carries a jailbreak root.
# A plist that already holds one therefore gets a second.
#
# The plist now ships with bare paths on roothide and launchctl supplies the
# root, which is what the two ordinary daemons in the bootstrap tarball do
# (us.diatr.shshd.plist names /bin/sh, com.apple.atrun.plist names
# /usr/libexec/atrun). The @JBROOT@ precedent came from a basebin daemon's own
# postinst, and it does not transfer: basebin daemons are loaded by the jailbreak
# itself through the native API rather than by the patched launchctl.
#
# Independent second reason: the jbroot identifier changes on every re-jailbreak.
# A baked-in absolute path expires at the next boot into a new bootstrap, and the
# daemon stops being found. Only the bare form survives that.
#
# This holds across the roothide family rather than for one jailbreak app. Both
# roothide Bootstrap and Dopamine's roothide fork ship byte-identical bootstrap
# tarballs, name the root .jbroot-<16 hex> from arc4random, and re-randomise it on
# every jailbreak by moving the tree. The reporting device runs the Dopamine fork.
#
# Consequence worth stating plainly: no dependency on plutil, on grep's binary
# behaviour, or on a textual rewrite of a length-prefixed binary plist. Those
# were three separate ways to corrupt or silently skip the file, and all three
# are gone with the substitution that needed them.

SCHEME_PREFIX='@PREFIX@'
NEEDS_JBROOT='@NEEDS_JBROOT@'
# What must literally appear inside the plist, decided at package time. Empty on
# roothide because launchctl prepends the root; /var/jb on rootless because
# nothing there rewrites anything. Handed to the guard so it can compare, not so
# it can open anything -- see the export block at the bottom.
LAUNCHD_PREFIX='@LAUNCHD_PREFIX@'

GUARD_RELATIVE='/usr/libexec/nrmanager-install-guard'
# Rendered from the same constants the plist was patched with, so the script and
# the file it loads cannot disagree about the label. A label copied by hand would
# not be caught by the contract check either: launchctl would load the plist and
# the verification would then ask launchd about a job nobody registered.
LAUNCHD_LABEL='@LAUNCHD_LABEL@'
# Carries the launchd prefix, because launchctl is what resolves this path, and on
# roothide launchctl applies its own redirection to it. Bare here for the same
# reason the plist is bare.
LAUNCHD_PLIST='@LAUNCHD_PLIST@'
# The only line the guard ever writes to stdout, and only when the installed
# plist matches the launchd contract. Everything the guard says to a human goes
# to stderr, so stdout carries this and nothing else.
#
# The shell must not load a job the guard has just reported as mismatched, and
# whether the shipped binary plist matches is the one question this script cannot
# answer for itself. Answering it here is what the retired plutil dependency was.
GUARD_LAUNCHD_VERIFIED='launchd-contract-verified'

note() {
    printf 'NR Manager: %s\n' "$1" >&2
}

warn() {
    printf 'NR Manager: warning — %s\n' "$1" >&2
}

# Printed on every exit path, including the early ones, because the reporting
# device twice produced a log whose last line was one of ours and whose install
# then appeared to stop -- and both times the question "is this script still
# running?" could not be answered from the log. It can be now: this line means
# the maintainer script returned, so anything still waiting afterwards is waiting
# on something else.
#
# A trap rather than a line before each exit, so a future early return cannot
# forget it. It leaves $? alone: the pending exit status is what the shell uses
# after the trap runs.
trap 'note "setup finished."' EXIT

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Candidate prefixes for reaching installed files from *this* process. At most
# two, and which applies is a property of the running process, so both are tried
# rather than guessed.
#
# roothide: the bare path first. If this shell does have the redirection then a
#           bare path is the correct one, and the jbroot-absolute path resolves
#           to the same file anyway.
# rootless: exactly one real prefix. A bare path would point outside the
#           jailbreak, so it is never a candidate.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# jbroot's output is an external input and is validated before use. A non-absolute
# or multi-line value must not be prepended to anything and must not be handed to
# the guard, which would then read a root that does not exist and find no policy
# records -- indistinguishable from a clean band configuration, the one
# conclusion the removal gate exists to refuse to reach by accident.
#
# Trailing slashes are normalised rather than rejected; the value is never
# normalised down to empty, because empty means "unresolved" everywhere else here.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
validate_jbroot() {
    case "$1" in
        '')
            printf 'jbroot produced no output'
            return 1
            ;;
        *'
'*)
            printf 'jbroot produced more than one line'
            return 1
            ;;
        /*)
            ;;
        *)
            printf 'jbroot produced a relative path'
            return 1
            ;;
    esac
    if [ ! -d "$1" ]; then
        printf 'jbroot named a path that is not a directory'
        return 1
    fi
    return 0
}

JBROOT_PREFIX=''
JBROOT_STATUS='not applicable to this install scheme'
if [ -n "$NEEDS_JBROOT" ]; then
    _jbroot="$(jbroot 2>/dev/null)"
    while :; do
        case "$_jbroot" in
            */) _jbroot="${_jbroot%/}" ;;
            *) break ;;
        esac
        [ -n "$_jbroot" ] || break
    done
    if _reason="$(validate_jbroot "$_jbroot")"; then
        JBROOT_PREFIX="$_jbroot"
        JBROOT_STATUS="$JBROOT_PREFIX"
    else
        JBROOT_STATUS="could not be resolved; ${_reason}"
    fi
    PREFIX_PRIMARY=''
    PREFIX_FALLBACK="$JBROOT_PREFIX"
else
    PREFIX_PRIMARY="$SCHEME_PREFIX"
    PREFIX_FALLBACK=''
fi

describe_prefix() {
    if [ -z "$1" ]; then
        printf 'the redirected root (bare paths)'
    else
        printf '%s' "$1"
    fi
}

candidate_list() {
    describe_prefix "$PREFIX_PRIMARY"
    if [ -n "$PREFIX_FALLBACK" ]; then
        printf ' and %s' "$PREFIX_FALLBACK"
    fi
}

# resolve() assigns RESOLVED_PATH and RESOLVED_PREFIX. It must not be called in a
# command substitution: that runs in a subshell and the caller would keep an
# empty RESOLVED_PREFIX while still getting a usable path, so every diagnostic
# would name the wrong prefix.
RESOLVED_PATH=''
RESOLVED_PREFIX=''
resolve() {
    _relative="$1"
    RESOLVED_PATH=''
    RESOLVED_PREFIX=''
    if [ -e "${PREFIX_PRIMARY}${_relative}" ]; then
        RESOLVED_PREFIX="$PREFIX_PRIMARY"
    elif [ -n "$PREFIX_FALLBACK" ] && [ -e "${PREFIX_FALLBACK}${_relative}" ]; then
        RESOLVED_PREFIX="$PREFIX_FALLBACK"
    else
        return 1
    fi
    RESOLVED_PATH="${RESOLVED_PREFIX}${_relative}"
    return 0
}

@LAUNCHCTL_SUPPORT@
LAUNCHD_TARGET="system/${LAUNCHD_LABEL}"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The policy guard owns everything that only reads and reports: clearing the
# removal guard when that is safe, and validating the launchd contract against
# the installed plist. Loading the job is this script's job, because a compiled
# guard cannot exec anything here -- see the launchctl support block above.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
if ! resolve "$GUARD_RELATIVE"; then
    warn "the install guard was not found under $(candidate_list), so band policy state was not validated and the maintenance owner was not started. jbroot: ${JBROOT_STATUS}."
    exit 0
fi
guard="$RESOLVED_PATH"

# Hand both prefixes over instead of letting the guard re-derive them. The guard
# is invoked through a bare path, so its own executable path carries no .jbroot-
# component and it has nothing to derive from.
#
# They answer two different questions and must not be merged:
#
#   NETWORKMANAGER_INSTALL_PREFIX  what the guard prepends to open a file. It is
#       not redirected -- it links no libroothide and is exec'd through a bare
#       path, so nothing rewrites its paths and a bare one lands on the real
#       root. It therefore needs the real jailbreak root, not the prefix this
#       shell resolved for itself. Exporting the latter was a real bug: this
#       shell's `/` already *is* the jailbreak root, so RESOLVED_PREFIX is
#       legitimately empty, and an empty prefix is a statement about this process
#       that must not cross a process boundary.
#
#   NETWORKMANAGER_LAUNCHD_PREFIX  what must literally appear inside the plist,
#       which the guard compares against the plist's contents. A package-time
#       constant, and legitimately empty on roothide.
#
# Empty and unset are different answers and the guard reads them differently, so
# the launchd prefix is always exported even when empty, while the install prefix
# is left unset when the jailbreak root is unknown. The guard then reports that it
# could not resolve anything and fails closed, instead of quietly reading the
# wrong root and calling it clean.
if [ -n "$NEEDS_JBROOT" ]; then
    if [ -n "$JBROOT_PREFIX" ]; then
        NETWORKMANAGER_INSTALL_PREFIX="$JBROOT_PREFIX"
        export NETWORKMANAGER_INSTALL_PREFIX
        note "handing the guard install prefix ${JBROOT_PREFIX} and launchd prefix $(describe_prefix "$LAUNCHD_PREFIX")."
    else
        warn "the jailbreak root ${JBROOT_STATUS}, so the guard was given no install prefix and will fail closed rather than read the wrong root."
    fi
else
    NETWORKMANAGER_INSTALL_PREFIX="$SCHEME_PREFIX"
    export NETWORKMANAGER_INSTALL_PREFIX
    note "handing the guard install prefix ${SCHEME_PREFIX} and launchd prefix ${LAUNCHD_PREFIX}."
fi
NETWORKMANAGER_LAUNCHD_PREFIX="$LAUNCHD_PREFIX"
export NETWORKMANAGER_LAUNCHD_PREFIX

# stdout is captured and stderr is not, so everything the guard says to a human
# still reaches the dpkg log in order, while the one machine-readable line it
# emits stays out of that log.
#
# stdin is closed for the same reason every launchctl call is detached: a
# maintainer-script child must not be able to sit on dpkg's descriptors. Here the
# capture already handles stdout, so only stdin needs saying.
guard_output="$("$guard" "$@" </dev/null)"
status=$?

launchd_verified=no
while IFS= read -r line; do
    if [ "$line" = "$GUARD_LAUNCHD_VERIFIED" ]; then
        launchd_verified=yes
    fi
done <<GUARD_OUTPUT
$guard_output
GUARD_OUTPUT

# A nonzero verdict is propagated unchanged and nothing is loaded. The guard's
# only nonzero exit is "not running as root", and launchctl would not work from
# there either.
if [ "$status" -ne 0 ]; then
    exit "$status"
fi

# The guard has already explained the mismatch on stderr. Loading a plist it just
# reported as not matching the contract would start something other than what was
# reviewed, so this is a hard stop for the load and a no-op for the install:
# configure still succeeds, because the package works without the daemon.
if [ "$launchd_verified" != yes ]; then
    warn "the maintenance owner was not started because the installed launchd job did not match the reviewed contract. Band policy changes work; automatic serving-state monitoring does not."
    exit 0
fi

if ! resolve_delay_command; then
    # Without a delay command no launchctl call can be bounded, and an unbounded
    # one is what hung the install. Skipping the immediate load costs only the
    # monitoring, and a later reinstall retries it -- see the note below on why a
    # reboot is not the recovery path here.
    note "the maintenance owner is installed but not started now: no usable sleep command was found, so launchctl could not be run under a deadline. Band policy changes work now. Reinstall the package to start automatic serving-state monitoring."
    exit 0
fi

# A reboot is deliberately not offered as the recovery path anywhere below.
#
# On a jailbreak whose root is re-randomised, a plain reboot ends the jailbreak
# altogether, and re-jailbreaking moves the whole tree to a new
# /var/containers/Bundle/Application/.jbroot-<16 hex>, so nothing here is loaded
# by launchd on the way back up: no jailbreak component walks this directory at
# boot, and the only loader for a package like this one is a maintainer script.
# "It starts at the next reboot" was therefore wrong advice, and the reporting
# device is what showed it -- four different jailbreak roots across one day.
#
# Both roothide-family jailbreaks say so directly. roothide Bootstrap has no
# boot-time walk of this directory at all, and Dopamine's roothide fork hooks
# launchd to inject <jbroot>/basebin/LaunchDaemons while keeping the equivalent
# block for <jbroot>/Library/LaunchDaemons commented out, annotated "should be
# loaded by procursus launchctl". That is exactly the loader this script is.
#
# The honest recovery is a reinstall, which runs this script again.
if ! resolve_launchctl; then
    # The plist is correct on disk and this package's own postinst is what loads
    # it, so only the immediate load was impossible. A notice, not a warning.
    note "the maintenance owner is installed but not running yet; launchctl could not be run${LAUNCHCTL_REPORT:+ (tried ${LAUNCHCTL_REPORT})}. Band policy changes work now; only automatic serving-state monitoring waits. Reinstall the package to start it."
    exit 0
fi

# Replace any definition left by a previous version, so the plist that ships with
# this one is the plist launchd holds. Failure is not conclusive and is not
# reported: on a first install there is nothing loaded to boot out.
launchd_bootout
bootout_status=$?
if [ "$bootout_status" -eq 1 ]; then
    # Not fatal, and not silent either. bootstrap returns EALREADY without
    # replacing a definition launchd already holds, so a surviving one means the
    # job launchd runs is the previous version's rather than this one's, and every
    # later check here reports it as loaded. Unrecoverable from inside this script.
    #
    # Deliberately not attributed to a stale jailbreak root. That explanation was
    # wrong: launchd is pid 1 and its system-domain job table does not survive a
    # reboot, and both ways the jailbreak root changes -- a reboot followed by
    # re-running the bootstrap app, or `launchctl reboot userspace` -- restart
    # launchd. A definition pointing into a root that no longer exists is therefore
    # only reachable inside one boot, which is exactly the case where the root did
    # not change. So this says what is known and nothing more.
    #
    # Only for status 1, which means the definition is known to have survived.
    # Status 2 means launchd stopped answering and nothing is known; the bounded
    # calls below report that themselves, and claiming a surviving definition on
    # that evidence would be inventing one.
    warn "a definition of the maintenance owner left by a previous install could not be removed, so launchd may keep running that one instead of this one. Band policy changes are unaffected. Installing the package again is what replaces it."
fi

launchctl_run "$LAUNCHCTL" bootstrap system "$LAUNCHD_PLIST"
bootstrap_status=$?
if [ "$bootstrap_status" -eq 124 ]; then
    # No verdict is available and none is invented: asking launchd again would
    # only spend another deadline, and the latch inside launchctl_run skips it
    # anyway. The job is deliberately not booted out -- launchd may well have
    # accepted the definition before the deadline.
    warn "launchd did not answer within ${LAUNCHCTL_DEADLINE_SECONDS}s and the request was stopped, so the maintenance owner may or may not be running. Band policy changes work now. If automatic serving-state monitoring turns out not to be running, reinstall the package."
    exit 0
fi
launchd_job_is_loaded
loaded_status=$?
if [ "$loaded_status" -eq 2 ]; then
    warn "launchd did not answer within ${LAUNCHCTL_DEADLINE_SECONDS}s when asked whether the maintenance owner loaded, so the request was stopped. Band policy changes work now. If automatic serving-state monitoring turns out not to be running, reinstall the package."
    exit 0
fi
if [ "$loaded_status" -ne 0 ]; then
    warn "the maintenance owner is installed but launchd declined to load it (bootstrap exit ${bootstrap_status}). Band policy changes work now. Automatic serving-state monitoring is unavailable until launchd accepts the job; reinstall the package to try again."
    exit 0
fi

# Success says so, because every other outcome above does. Without this line a
# clean install is reported only by the absence of warnings, and a user asked what
# the silence meant -- a fair question the log should not have needed.
#
# Worded as loaded rather than running, which is what `print` actually confirmed.
# The plist starts the job at load and whenever the preferences directory
# changes, so launchd runs the job while a band policy is saved for the current
# data line and lets it stay down when none is.
#
# Suppressed when a previous definition is known to have survived: launchd answers
# "loaded" either way, and after that warning this line would read as though the
# loaded job were this package's. The warning is the accurate report there.
if [ "$bootout_status" -ne 1 ]; then
    note "the maintenance owner is loaded. It runs while a band policy is saved."
fi

# Deliberately no kickstart here.
#
# It was the only call that ever hit the deadline on the reporting device, and the
# reason is specific: `kickstart` waits for the job to come up. That device's
# `launchctl print` showed the job at `state = spawn scheduled` with
# `minimum runtime = 1200` against a `base minimum runtime = 30`, the exponential
# backoff launchd had built from 108 consecutive failed launches. Waiting for a
# start inside that window means waiting out the window -- up to twenty minutes
# with the package manager's pipe still held open.
#
# It also buys nothing this script does not already have. The plist starts the
# job at load and whenever the preferences directory changes, so the moment a
# band policy is saved launchd has every reason to run the job -- including
# right now, because bootstrapping a job whose start conditions are already
# satisfied starts it. The bootout above
# already guarantees the running instance is this package's binary rather than a
# previous one. So kickstart only ever meant "and start it a second time", at the
# cost of a call that can block the package manager and a ThrottleInterval-delayed
# restart when it lands on a job launchd had just started.
#
# This also means the bootstrap above is the only thing that ever starts the
# maintenance owner. Nothing in the jailbreak walks this LaunchDaemons directory
# at boot, and a re-jailbreak moves the tree to a fresh jailbreak root, so if the
# load above did not happen, the recovery is another install, not a reboot.

exit 0
